        "heading": "How boat names work",
        "content": "<p>Most boat names follow one of a few old habits:</p><ul><li><strong>A person.</strong> Boats have long been called \"she\", and plenty are named after a wife, mother or daughter. In the West Country it's common to name a fishing boat Girl, Boy or Our followed by a name, so Girl Rosie or Our Tom.</li><li><strong>A pun.</strong> Sea words bend easily: reel and real, knot and not, sea and see, pier and peer. Reel Therapy, Knot on Call and Pier Pressure are favourites for a reason. Plenty of boat puns have a rude second meaning, so this generator leaves those out.</li><li><strong>The water itself.</strong> Wind, tide, birds and stars give sailing names like Restless Gull or Song of the Isles.</li><li><strong>A place.</strong> Adding a harbour town, as in Aurelia of Fowey, turns a common name into one nobody else has.</li></ul><p>Short names are easiest to paint, read from the bank and say clearly over a radio. Two or three words is plenty.</p>"
